---
description: Start every task with a BDD + mutation-testing verification contract
alwaysApply: true
---

# Verification contract first

Before writing implementation, write a **verification contract**. Do not code until invariants, edge cases, and QA commands are listed. Do not call the work done until every invariant passes and scoped mutants are killed.

## Contract (write this first)

```
Goal: <observable outcome; full requested scope>
Invariants:
  I1 <behavior> -> tests/<file>::<case>
  I2 ...
Edge cases: <empty, invalid, auth, concurrency, replay, limits — only valuable ones>
Out of scope: <explicit non-goals>
QA:
  - bun run check
  - <BDD tests for this change>
  - <mutation run scoped to changed production files>
```

Every user-facing requirement maps to at least one invariant. If a requirement has no test name, the contract is incomplete.

## Tests: BDD, then mutate

Write tests as **Given / When / Then** on observable behavior (HTTP, CLI, UI, store effects). Cover the valuable edge cases in the contract. Do not test implementation details that a refactor would break without changing behavior.

```
# ❌ BAD — starts coding; asserts internals
export function add(a, b) { return a + b }
it("calls add", () => expect(add(1, 2)).toBe(3))

# ✅ GOOD — contract + behavior + edges
# I1 send is idempotent for the same key -> tests/api.test.ts::retries
it("given the same Idempotency-Key, when send is retried, then the stored message is not duplicated")
```

Run a **mutation test** on the production files this task changes (StrykerJS / `@stryker-mutator/vitest-runner` unless the repo already has a mutator). Surviving mutants mean missing assertions: kill them or document an equivalent mutant. A green suite with live mutants is not done.
